cleaned_proof:
Let n=1, \sum_{i=0}^1i=0+1=1= rac{1*(1+1)}{2} 
ewline\ Suppose the statement is true when n=k, while k is an integer, then, \sum_{i=0}^{k}i= rac{k(k+1)}{2}\ is true \newline\ Let n=k+1, then \LHS=\sum_{i=0}^{k+1}i=\sum_{i=0}^{k}i+(k+1)= rac{k(k+1)}{2}+(k+1)=( rac{k}{2}+1)*(k+1)= rac{(k+1)(k+2)}{2}
ewline\ \because n = k+1, 
ewline 	herefore LHS= rac{n(n+1)}{2}=RHS\ Since the statement is true when n=1, n=k+1 is true while n=k is true for any interger k, the statement is true due to the mathematical induction.
